\section{Implementation Details}
\label{app:implementation}

This appendix gives the implementation details omitted from \cref{sec:method}.
We first describe the successor networks and the two-head implementation, then the policy extractor and zero-shot evaluation procedure.
Environment-specific outcome coordinates and all hyperparameters are given last.


\subsection{Successor networks}
\label{app:networks}

The forward and backward networks are MLPs.
Each hidden layer uses \textsc{Linear}$\rightarrow$\textsc{GELU}$\rightarrow$\textsc{LayerNorm}, followed by a linear output layer.

The backward network $B_\theta$ has one output head and no output normalization or orthogonality regularizer.
The forward network receives $[s,a,z]$ and has two heads,
\begin{equation}
    F_\theta(s,a,z) \in \mathbb R^{2\times d}.
\end{equation}

Each forward head defines its own normalized successor distribution using Equation~\eqref{eq:density-model}.
The representation loss in Equation~\eqref{eq:density-loss} is computed independently for the two heads and then averaged.

Target networks $\bar F$ and $\bar B$ are updated after every gradient step using an exponential moving average with rate $\eta$.
The bootstrap action is produced by the current actor and treated as a stop-gradient when constructing the successor target.

\paragraph{Combining the two heads.}
Each forward head $h\in\{1,2\}$ defines its own normalized distribution $p^{(h)}(s,a,z)$ over the candidate next states, and each head is trained against its own target.
The two heads are combined only after a reward has been integrated: for a reward $r$ the per-head values are $q^{(h)}(s,a,z)=\sum_j p^{(h)}_j(s,a,z)\,r(s'_j)$ and the value used by the actor is
\begin{equation}
    q(s,a,z) := \min\left\{ q^{(1)}(s,a,z),\, q^{(2)}(s,a,z) \right\}. \label{eq:app-double-q}
\end{equation}
Because each head remains a probability distribution, adding a constant to the reward adds the same constant to both heads' values and hence to $q$, so action rankings are invariant to reward shifts; taking the minimum of the two probability vectors instead would not have this property, since its total mass depends on the action.
The normalization and fixed-point results of \cref{sec:method} apply to each head.

\subsection{Policy extraction}
\label{app:actor}

The learned successor distribution provides the action value used for policy improvement.
For each forward head $h$, we compute
\begin{equation}
    q^{(h)}(s,a,z) := \sum_{j=1}^{n} p_j^{(h)}(s,a,z) r_z(s'_j). \label{eq:app-head-value}
\end{equation}

The value used by the actor is the minimum of the two heads' values, $q:=\min_h q^{(h)}$, as in \cref{eq:app-double-q}.
We normalize its scale using
\begin{equation}
    s_q := \sg\left[ \E\left| q-\E[q] \right| + \varepsilon \right], \qquad \varepsilon=10^{-6}. \label{eq:app-value-scale}
\end{equation}

This normalization controls the scale of the value term relative to the behavior-regularization term.


\paragraph{Flow-based actor.}

For the main experiments, we use the flow-based policy extractor used in TD-JEPA~\citep{bagatella-iclr26a}, based on Flow Q-Learning~\citep{park-icml25a}.

A goal-agnostic velocity field $v_\psi(s,a,t)$ models the behavior policy.
Given
\begin{equation}
    x_0\sim\mathcal N(0,I), \qquad x_1=a, \qquad t\sim\mathcal U[0,1],
\end{equation}
we define
\begin{equation}
    x_t := (1-t)x_0+t x_1,
\end{equation}
and train the velocity field with
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal L_{\mathrm{flow}} := \E \left[ \left\| v_\psi(s,x_t,t) - (x_1-x_0) \right\|^2 \right]. \label{eq:app-flow-loss}
\end{equation}

Let
\begin{equation}
    a_{\mathrm{BC}} := \mathrm{ODE}_\psi(s,\eta), \qquad \eta\sim\mathcal N(0,I),
\end{equation}
denote the action obtained by integrating the learned velocity field for $K$ Euler steps.

The one-step actor $\pi_\omega(s,z;\eta)$ is trained using
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal L_{\pi} := - \frac{ \E\left[ q\left( s, \pi_\omega(s,z;\eta), z \right) \right] }{ s_q } + \lambda_{\mathrm{BC}} \E \left[ \left\| \pi_\omega(s,z;\eta) - a_{\mathrm{BC}} \right\|^2 \right]. \label{eq:app-flow-actor}
\end{equation}

The velocity field and the actor are optimized separately using Equations~\eqref{eq:app-flow-loss} and~\eqref{eq:app-flow-actor}.
Removing the value term from Equation~\eqref{eq:app-flow-actor} gives the corresponding behavior-cloning policy.

During the actor update, the successor networks are fixed.
Gradients pass through the action input of $F_\theta$ to the actor, but the parameters of $F_\theta$ and $B_\theta$ are not updated.


\paragraph{DDPG+BC actor.}

For the alternative extractor, we use a Gaussian policy
\begin{equation}
    \pi_\omega(\cdot\mid s,z) = \mathcal N \left( \mu_\omega(s,z), \sigma^2 I \right),
\end{equation}
with fixed standard deviation $\sigma$.

Its objective is
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal L_{\pi} = - \frac{ \E\left[ q(s,\mu_\omega(s,z),z) \right] }{ s_q } - \alpha \E \left[ \log \pi_\omega(a\mid s,z) \right]. \label{eq:app-ddpgbc}
\end{equation}


\subsection{Reward inference and zero-shot evaluation}
\label{app:zeroshot-protocol}
\label{app:reward-inference}

All zero-shot evaluations use frozen successor and policy networks.
A downstream reward is introduced only after pretraining, and no gradient updates or additional environment interaction are performed.

For reward inference, we evaluate the downstream reward on $10^4$ states sampled from the offline dataset and infer the corresponding task vector using the procedure described in \cref{sec:method-control}.
\aditya{State the exact regression used for $z_r$ here so that this matches the main method exactly.}

To evaluate an action value, we sample a fixed pool
\begin{equation}
    U_P = \{u_1,\ldots,u_P\}
\end{equation}
from the dataset next-state marginal $\rho$ and precompute $B_\theta(u_j)$.
For a reward $r$, the finite-pool estimate is
\begin{equation}
    \hat q_{\theta,r}(s,a,z) = \sum_{j=1}^{P} p_j(s,a,z) r(u_j). \label{eq:app-zs-readout}
\end{equation}

The pool is sampled once using a fixed seed and reused throughout evaluation, with $P=4096$; $p_j$ is the combination of \cref{eq:app-double-q} applied to the pool.
These pool values are used only for the diagnostics of \cref{sec:exp-failure}: at deployment the agent executes the frozen actor $\pi_\omega(\cdot\mid s,z_r)$ on the inferred task vector, as in Algorithm~1, and no value is computed.
Direct goal conditioning is likewise a diagnostic: it uses the goal supplied by the environment to specify the task and therefore removes reward-to-task inference from the evaluation.
All network parameters remain frozen in both evaluation modes.

