\subsection{Sampling task vectors for critic and actor training}
\label{app:z-sampling}

Every training row of the critic and of the actor carries a task vector $z\in\mathbb R^d$ with $\|z\|=\sqrt d$; the forward network conditions on it, and for the actor it defines the reward the policy ascends.
How $z$ is drawn is a design decision, since the policy family is only trained on the task vectors it sees.
We use FB's standard task-prior mix with one change in how the goal-derived half is produced.
For each row, with probability $1-p_{\mathrm{rand}}$ the task vector is the encoding of a hindsight goal: a future state of the row's own trajectory is drawn as the goal, the goal-reaching reward it defines (the hit kernel of the successor objective) is evaluated on the batch's candidate next states, and $z$ is the ridge regression of that reward on the centered backward features of \cref{app:task-index}, computed with a slowly updated copy of the backward network and the running mean and covariance of its features.
With probability $p_{\mathrm{rand}}$ the task vector is instead a fresh draw from the uniform distribution on the sphere of radius $\sqrt d$, so that the actor is trained across the whole conditioning space and not only on the slice reachable by hindsight goals.
Both branches are treated as constants: no gradient flows into the backward network through $z$.
The same encoding is used at test time on the reward samples of the downstream task, so the goal-derived task vectors seen in training and the inferred task vectors seen at evaluation come from one map.

The actor's reward for a task vector is the centered latent reward $r_z(s^+)=(B_\xi(s^+)-\hat\mu)^\top z/\sqrt d$, read with the same slow copy $B_\xi$ and running mean $\hat\mu$ that produced $z$, so that the reward the actor ascends is the one the encoder fitted; reading it from the fast target network instead trains the actor against a drifting reward and lost most of the cube-single success in our ablations.
For rows that kept a hindsight goal the exact goal kernel can be used in place of the latent reward; on \texttt{cube-single} this made no difference to success and we keep the latent reward throughout.

The fraction $p_{\mathrm{rand}}$ is the one knob of this mix.
The manipulation results use $p_{\mathrm{rand}}=1/2$, FB's default.
On ExoRL the tasks are dense velocity rewards whose task vectors lie far from every hindsight goal's, and drawing every training task vector from the sphere ($p_{\mathrm{rand}}=1$) raised \texttt{walker} and \texttt{cheetah} while lowering \texttt{quadruped} and \texttt{point-mass}; the per-domain choice is recorded in \cref{app:exorl-hparams}.

\subsection{ExoRL settings per domain}
\label{app:exorl-hparams}

The ExoRL cells of \cref{tab:benchmarks} use, for every domain, the RND datasets, state observations, $10^6$ gradient steps, five seeds, and task inference from $5\times10^4$ reward-labelled transitions, and the settings of \cref{tab:hparams} except as listed here.
Shared across domains: successor towers $(512,512,512)$, backward towers $(256,256)$ with the output projected onto the sphere, target rate $0.005$, learning rate $10^{-4}$, actor learning rate $10^{-4}$, discount $0.98$, and a hit kernel over the full observation with the radius calibrated to the hit rate of the manipulation kernel (\texttt{walker} $0:24$ at $15.9$, \texttt{quadruped} $0:78$ at $21.1$).
Per domain: \texttt{point-mass} uses discount $0.99$, actor learning rate $10^{-6}$ and a kernel over the two position coordinates at radius $0.03$, as every reference implementation does on this domain; \texttt{walker} uses task vectors drawn only from the sphere during training, the actor's value normalizer, a single successor tower of width $1024$ and target rate $0.001$, which raised its return from $724.6$ to $750.2$ and lowered \texttt{quadruped} and \texttt{point-mass}, so those two keep the shared base recipe; \texttt{quadruped} additionally restricts the hit kernel to the three torso-velocity coordinates (columns $44$--$46$, radius $0.11$) and the torso-upright coordinate (column $47$, radius $0.001$), which raised its return from $655.9$ to $760.5$; \texttt{cheetah} uses the \texttt{walker} settings with the hit kernel restricted to the nine velocity coordinates of the observation (columns $8$--$16$, radius $7.26$ at the same hit rate), which raised its return from $431$ to $540$, against $532$ for a kernel over the forward-speed coordinate alone.
The selection was made on the reported seeds.

